{
  // Everything in this file is public: it is committed. The API key is NOT here.
  // It goes in as a secret, which is encrypted at rest and never shown again:
  //
  //     npx wrangler secret put HELIUS_API_KEY
  //
  // A key pasted into this file would be in git history, which is the exact
  // problem this worker exists to solve, one layer further down.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "commish-rpc",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-09-07",

  /* THE CUSTOM DOMAIN, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES RATE LIMITING POSSIBLE AT ALL.
     Deploying with this provisions rpc.commish.fun — DNS record and
     certificate — inside the commish.fun zone. That matters because the
     `ratelimits` binding further down is inert on *.workers.dev, which
     Cloudflare treats as a Free website outside your zones, and a WAF Rate
     Limiting Rule can only be attached to a zone. So the domain is the
     prerequisite for the one control that actually works.

     AND CLOSING THE OLD URL IS HALF THE FIX. `workers_dev` and `preview_urls`
     both default to TRUE, so adding this domain does not retire
     commish-rpc.<subdomain>.workers.dev — it runs alongside. A WAF rule on the
     zone does nothing to traffic that arrives at the workers.dev hostname, so
     leaving it up leaves the rule bypassable by anyone who knows the old URL,
     which is anyone who ever read the bundle. The rule would look configured
     and enforce nothing, which is this repository's most familiar failure.

     THE ORDER MATTERS, because getting it wrong takes the site down. Flipping
     workers_dev to false while Vercel still points NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL at the
     old hostname breaks every page that talks to the chain. So:

       1. deploy with this route, both hostnames live
       2. set NEXT_PUBLIC_RPC_URL = https://rpc.commish.fun in Vercel, redeploy,
          and confirm a pool page still loads
       3. only then uncomment the two lines below and deploy again
       4. add the WAF Rate Limiting Rule on the zone

     Steps 1 and 2 are safe at any time. Step 3 is the one that must come
     second, and it is why these are left commented rather than set. */
  "routes": [{ "pattern": "rpc.commish.fun", "custom_domain": true }],
  // "workers_dev": false,
  // "preview_urls": false,

  // Logs go to `wrangler tail`. The worker logs no request bodies and no
  // credentials, so this carries method names and status codes, which is what
  // you want when a screen starts calling something the allowlist refuses.
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },

  "vars": {
    // devnet or mainnet. Defaulted in code as well, because a proxy that
    // quietly talks to mainnet because a variable was unset is a worse failure
    // than one that talks to the wrong test cluster.
    "HELIUS_CLUSTER": "devnet",

    // The origins allowed to use this proxy, compared whole rather than by
    // suffix. www is still here because Vercel still answers on it, as a
    // redirect; harmless, and it costs nothing to keep until that is retired.
    //
    // There is no wildcard and no default. The worker refuses every request
    // while this is empty, on purpose: an open proxy holding a live key is the
    // one thing that must not be what happens when somebody forgets a
    // variable.
    "CORS_ALLOW_ORIGIN": "https://commish.fun,https://www.commish.fun",

    // The one program getProgramAccounts may scan.
    //
    // THIS WAS COMMENTED OUT, ON THE REASONING THAT "the id belongs with the
    // deploy, not here", to be supplied as `--var PROGRAM_ID:<id>` at cutover.
    // That reasoning was wrong twice over.
    //
    // It was wrong in fact: the id is a constant. It comes from
    // target/deploy/commish-keypair.json, it is `declare_id!` in lib.rs, it is
    // baked into src/idl/commish.json, and it is the SAME on devnet and
    // mainnet. There is no per-deploy value to supply.
    //
    // And it was wrong in effect, which is the part that was measured rather
    // than argued. With it unset the guard in index.ts:153 is skipped entirely
    // and only the weaker "must carry filters" test at :156 runs. Probed
    // against the live worker on 2026-09-08, forging nothing but an Origin
    // header:
    //
    //   getProgramAccounts, someone else's program, no filters -> 403
    //   getProgramAccounts, someone else's program, WITH a filter -> 200 []
    //
    // So the deployed proxy would scan any program on Solana for anybody who
    // attached a dataSize filter, on our Helius key, with no working rate limit
    // in front of it. A protection that depends on somebody remembering a CLI
    // flag during a cutover is not a protection; it is a plan to have one.
    //
    // Here it survives every deploy. The `--var` in mainnet-cutover.sh still
    // passes the same value and simply agrees with this line.
    "PROGRAM_ID": "Adb5CFrY4vYiGQnTQ5qsaPPMUAWKyxwVAQtFHWcshjPa"
  },

  /* RATE LIMITING — AND IT DOES NOT CURRENTLY WORK. READ THIS BEFORE TRUSTING
     THIS ENDPOINT.

     The bindings below attach correctly: `wrangler deploy --dry-run` lists
     them, a deployed worker sees RPC_LIMIT as an object, and env.RPC_LIMIT
     .limit() is callable and returns a result. It simply never denies.

     Measured, on a preview version uploaded with the limit dropped to 5 per 10
     seconds so the threshold could not be missed:
       - 15 sequential POSTs      -> 15 x 200, no 429
       - 10 websocket upgrades    -> 10 x 101, no 429
       - 12 direct limit() calls on ONE key -> 12 x {"success":true}

     The last line is the decisive one: the limiter itself returns success
     twelve times against a budget of five. This is not a keying mistake, not
     the documented per-location counter, and not eventual consistency at the
     margin. The binding is inert on this worker.

     The likeliest cause is that this Worker is served from *.workers.dev,
     which Cloudflare treats as a Free website outside your zones. THE FIX IS
     THEREFORE NOT IN THIS FILE: put the worker on a custom domain inside the
     commish.fun zone (rpc.commish.fun) and apply a WAF Rate Limiting Rule,
     which is a dashboard change. The config below is kept because it is
     correct and costs nothing, and because it will start working the moment
     the platform lets it — but until the numbers above come back with 429s in
     them, this endpoint is NOT rate limited.

     Two budgets, because a POST and a socket are not worth the same upstream:
     a POST is billed once, a subscription is billed for as long as it is held.

     The origin check above is a single header and anyone can
     send it — this repo forges exactly that header itself, deliberately, in
     src/lib/leaderboard.ts. So the origin list is a convention and this is the
     actual control on a public URL that spends the owner's Helius credits.

     Two budgets, because a POST and a socket are not worth the same upstream:
     a POST is billed once, a subscription is billed for as long as it is held.

     THE NUMBERS ARE DELIBERATELY GENEROUS. One pool page fans out into several
     account reads plus a socket, and a household, an office or a campus all
     share one cf-connecting-ip behind NAT. Watch `wrangler tail` for 429s
     before tightening these. Note the binding's counter is approximate and
     per-location rather than global, so treat these as an order of magnitude
     rather than an exact ceiling. */
  "ratelimits": [
    {
      "name": "RPC_LIMIT",
      "namespace_id": "1001",
      "simple": { "limit": 300, "period": 60 }
    },
    {
      "name": "WS_LIMIT",
      "namespace_id": "1002",
      "simple": { "limit": 30, "period": 60 }
    }
  ]
}


